<div><img width="800" height="484" src="https://hackaday.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/03/Browser.jpg?w=800" class="attachment-large size-large wp-post-image" alt="" style="margin: 0 auto; margin-bottom: 15px;" decoding="async" loading="lazy" srcset="https://hackaday.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/03/Browser.jpg 3000w, https://hackaday.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/03/Browser.jpg?resize=250,151 250w, https://hackaday.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/03/Browser.jpg?resize=400,242 400w, https://hackaday.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/03/Browser.jpg?resize=800,484 800w, https://hackaday.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/03/Browser.jpg?resize=1536,929 1536w, https://hackaday.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/03/Browser.jpg?resize=2048,1239 2048w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 800px) 100vw, 800px" data-attachment-id="770760" data-permalink="https://hackaday.com/2025/04/07/which-browser-should-i-use-in-2025/browser/" data-orig-file="https://hackaday.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/03/Browser.jpg" data-orig-size="3000,1815" data-comments-opened="1" data-image-meta="{&quot;aperture&quot;:&quot;0&quot;,&quot;credit&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;camera&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;caption&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;created_timestamp&quot;:&quot;0&quot;,&quot;copyright&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;focal_length&quot;:&quot;0&quot;,&quot;iso&quot;:&quot;0&quot;,&quot;shutter_speed&quot;:&quot;0&quot;,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;orientation&quot;:&quot;1&quot;}" data-image-title="Browser" data-image-description="" data-image-caption="" data-medium-file="https://hackaday.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/03/Browser.jpg?w=400" data-large-file="https://hackaday.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/03/Browser.jpg?w=800" /></div><p>It&#8217;s been twenty-three years since the day Phoenix was released, the web browser that eventually became Firefox. I downloaded it on the first day and installed it on my trusty HP Omnibook 800 laptop, and until this year I&#8217;ve used it ever since. Yet after all this time, I&#8217;m ready to abandon it for another browser. <a href="https://hackaday.com/2025/11/20/so-long-firefox-part-one/">In the previous article in this series</a> I went into my concerns over the direction being taken by Mozilla with respect to their inclusion of AI features and my worries about privacy in Firefox, and I explained why a plurality of browser engines is important for the Web. Now it&#8217;s time to follow me on my search for a replacement, and you may be surprised by one aspect of my eventual choice.</p>
<h2>Where Do I Go From Here?</h2>
<figure id="attachment_624102" aria-describedby="caption-attachment-624102" style="width: 400px" class="wp-caption alignright"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" data-attachment-id="624102" data-permalink="https://hackaday.com/2023/10/10/jennys-daily-drivers-serenityos-and-in-particular-ladybird/ladybird-linux-hackaday/" data-orig-file="https://hackaday.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/09/ladybird-linux-hackaday.jpg" data-orig-size="1416,942" data-comments-opened="1" data-image-meta="{&quot;aperture&quot;:&quot;0&quot;,&quot;credit&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;camera&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;caption&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;created_timestamp&quot;:&quot;0&quot;,&quot;copyright&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;focal_length&quot;:&quot;0&quot;,&quot;iso&quot;:&quot;0&quot;,&quot;shutter_speed&quot;:&quot;0&quot;,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;orientation&quot;:&quot;0&quot;}" data-image-title="ladybird-linux-hackaday" data-image-description="" data-image-caption="" data-medium-file="https://hackaday.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/09/ladybird-linux-hackaday.jpg?w=400" data-large-file="https://hackaday.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/09/ladybird-linux-hackaday.jpg?w=800" class="wp-image-624102 size-medium" src="https://hackaday.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/09/ladybird-linux-hackaday.jpg?w=400" alt="Hackaday in the Ladybird browser" width="400" height="266" srcset="https://hackaday.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/09/ladybird-linux-hackaday.jpg 1416w, https://hackaday.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/09/ladybird-linux-hackaday.jpg?resize=250,166 250w, https://hackaday.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/09/ladybird-linux-hackaday.jpg?resize=400,266 400w, https://hackaday.com/wp-content/uploads/2023/09/ladybird-linux-hackaday.jpg?resize=800,532 800w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 400px) 100vw, 400px" /><figcaption id="caption-attachment-624102" class="wp-caption-text">It&#8217;s Hackaday, in Ladybird! (Ooof, that font.)</figcaption></figure>
<p>Happily for my own purposes, there are a range of Firefox alternatives which fulfill my browser needs without AI cruft and while allowing me to be a little more at peace with my data security and privacy. There&#8217;s <a href="https://www.chromium.org/Home/" target="_blank">Chromium</a> of course even if it&#8217;s still way too close to Google for my liking, and there are a host of open-source WebKit and Blink based browsers too numerous to name here.</p>
<p>In the Gecko world that should be an easier jump for a Firefox escapee there are also several choices, for example <a href="https://librewolf.net"style="cursor: pointer !important; user-select: none !important;"  target="_blank">LibreWolf</a>, and <a href="https://www.waterfox.net"style="cursor: pointer !important; user-select: none !important;"  target="_blank">Waterfox</a>. In terms of other browser engines there&#8217;s the extremely promising but still early in development <a href="https://ladybird.org" target="_blank">Ladybird</a>, and the more mature <a href="https://servo.org" target="_blank">Servo</a>, which though it is available as a no-frills browser, bills itself as an embedded browser engine. I have not considered some other projects that are either lightweight browser engines, or ones not under significant active development.<span id="more-880686"></span></p>
<figure id="attachment_880699" aria-describedby="caption-attachment-880699" style="width: 400px" class="wp-caption alignleft"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" data-attachment-id="880699" data-permalink="https://hackaday.com/2025/11/25/so-long-firefox-part-two/hackaday-in-servo/" data-orig-file="https://hackaday.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/hackaday-in-servo.jpg" data-orig-size="1280,989" data-comments-opened="1" data-image-meta="{&quot;aperture&quot;:&quot;0&quot;,&quot;credit&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;camera&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;caption&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;created_timestamp&quot;:&quot;0&quot;,&quot;copyright&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;focal_length&quot;:&quot;0&quot;,&quot;iso&quot;:&quot;0&quot;,&quot;shutter_speed&quot;:&quot;0&quot;,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;orientation&quot;:&quot;0&quot;}" data-image-title="hackaday-in-servo" data-image-description="" data-image-caption="" data-medium-file="https://hackaday.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/hackaday-in-servo.jpg?w=400" data-large-file="https://hackaday.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/hackaday-in-servo.jpg?w=800" class="wp-image-880699 size-medium" src="https://hackaday.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/hackaday-in-servo.jpg?w=400" alt="A screenshot of Hackaday in the servo browser engine." width="400" height="309" srcset="https://hackaday.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/hackaday-in-servo.jpg 1280w, https://hackaday.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/hackaday-in-servo.jpg?resize=250,193 250w, https://hackaday.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/hackaday-in-servo.jpg?resize=400,309 400w, https://hackaday.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/hackaday-in-servo.jpg?resize=800,618 800w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 400px) 100vw, 400px" /><figcaption id="caption-attachment-880699" class="wp-caption-text">It&#8217;s Hackaday, in Servo!</figcaption></figure>
<p>Over this summer and autumn then I have tried a huge number of different browsers. Every month or so I build the latest Ladybird and Servo; while I am hugely pleased to see progress they&#8217;re both still too buggy for my purposes. Servo is lightning-fast but sometimes likes to get stuck in mobile view, while Ladybird is really showing what it&#8217;s going to be but remains for now slow-as-treacle. These are ones to watch, and support.</p>
<p>I gave LibreWolf and Waterfox the most attention over the summer, both of which after the experience I&#8217;d describe as like Firefox but with mildly annoying bugs. The inability to video conference reliably is a show-stopper in my line of work, and since my eyesight is no longer what it once was I like my browsers to remember when I have zoomed in on a tab. Meanwhile Waterfox on Android is a great mobile browser, right up until it needs to open a link in another app, and fails. I&#8217;m used to the quirks of open-source software after 30+ years experimenting with Linux, but when it comes to productivity I can&#8217;t let my software disrupt the flow of Hackaday articles.</p>
<h2>The Unexpected Choice</h2>
<figure id="attachment_880700" aria-describedby="caption-attachment-880700" style="width: 400px" class="wp-caption alignright"><a href="https://hackaday.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/hackaday-in-vivaldi.jpg" target="_blank"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" data-attachment-id="880700" data-permalink="https://hackaday.com/2025/11/25/so-long-firefox-part-two/hackaday-in-vivaldi/" data-orig-file="https://hackaday.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/hackaday-in-vivaldi.jpg" data-orig-size="1280,1024" data-comments-opened="1" data-image-meta="{&quot;aperture&quot;:&quot;0&quot;,&quot;credit&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;camera&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;caption&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;created_timestamp&quot;:&quot;0&quot;,&quot;copyright&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;focal_length&quot;:&quot;0&quot;,&quot;iso&quot;:&quot;0&quot;,&quot;shutter_speed&quot;:&quot;0&quot;,&quot;title&quot;:&quot;&quot;,&quot;orientation&quot;:&quot;0&quot;}" data-image-title="hackaday-in-vivaldi" data-image-description="" data-image-caption="" data-medium-file="https://hackaday.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/hackaday-in-vivaldi.jpg?w=400" data-large-file="https://hackaday.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/hackaday-in-vivaldi.jpg?w=781" class="wp-image-880700 size-medium" src="https://hackaday.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/hackaday-in-vivaldi.jpg?w=400" alt="A screenshot of Hackaday, in the Vivaldi browser." width="400" height="320" srcset="https://hackaday.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/hackaday-in-vivaldi.jpg 1280w, https://hackaday.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/hackaday-in-vivaldi.jpg?resize=250,200 250w, https://hackaday.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/hackaday-in-vivaldi.jpg?resize=400,320 400w, https://hackaday.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/11/hackaday-in-vivaldi.jpg?resize=781,625 781w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 400px) 100vw, 400px" /></a><figcaption id="caption-attachment-880700" class="wp-caption-text">It&#8217;s Hackaday, in Vivaldi!</figcaption></figure>
<p>It might surprise you after all this open-source enthusiasm then, to see the browser I&#8217;ve ended up comfortable with. <a href="https://vivaldi.com" target="_blank">Vivaldi</a> may be driven by the open-source Blink engine from Chromium and Chrome, but its proprietary front end doesn&#8217;t have an open-source licence.</p>
<p>It&#8217;s freeware, or free-as-in-beer, and I think the only such software I use. Why, I hear you ask? It&#8217;s an effort to produce a browser like Opera used to be in the old days, it&#8217;s European which is a significant consideration when it comes to data protection law, and it has (so far) maintained a commitment to privacy while not being evil in the Google motto sense.</p>
<p>It&#8217;s quick, I like its interface once the garish coloured default theme has been turned off, and above all, it Just Works. I have my browser back, and I can get on with writing. Should they turn evil I can dump them without a second thought, and hope by then Ladybird has matured enough to suit my needs.</p>
<p>It may not be a trend many of us particularly like, but here in 2025 there&#8217;s a sense that the browser has reduced our computers almost to the status of a terminal. It&#8217;s thus perhaps the most important piece of software on the device, and in that light I hope you can understand some of the concerns levelled in this series. If you&#8217;re reading this from Firefox HQ I&#8217;d implore you to follow my advice and go back to what made Firefox so great back in the day, but for the rest of you I&#8217;d like to canvass your views on my choice of a worthy replacement. As always, the comments are waiting.</p>
